  .  .  .  .  . 100\nEnglish  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 101\nFilm History  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 101\nFilm   . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 103\nFoundation  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 106\nGraduate Research  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 106\nJewellery   . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 107\nMaster of Arts in Education  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 111\nMaster of Design  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 114\nMedia Arts   . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 116\nMaster Of Fine Arts  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 120\nPhotography  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 124\nPainting   . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 127\nPrintmaking  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 131\nSculpture  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 133\nT extiles  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  138\nWorld T ravel  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 143\nCALENDAR\n26-27\nACADEMIC INFO\n+ COURSE PLANNING\nNSCAD  UNIVERSITY  76   ACADEMIC CATALOGUE  2024/25\nFall \n2026\nFall Semester\nSeptember 04\nSeptember 08\nSeptember 22\nOctober 06\nSeptember  30\nOctober 12\nOctober 15\nOctober 21\nNovember 03\nNovember 11\nNovember 09-13\nDecember 08\nDecember 09 \nDecember 10-16\nOrientation - New Students \nSemester classes begin \nLast day to add/ drop a course\nwithout financial impact \nUniversity Closed - T ruth & Reconciliation Day\nCourses dropped recorded on transcript \nUniversity Closed - Thanksgiving \nAdministrative graduation date for students who \ncompleted their degree requirements\nin Summer 2026 (ceremony hosted in May 2027) \nFaculty mid-semester warning deadline\nLast day to drop course \nUniversity Closed – Remembrance Day \nNo classes - Fall Study Break \nMonday classes run in place of Tuesday\nLast Day of Classes \nAssessment period (flex/study/exams/ critiques) \nLast day for grade submissions (noon) December 18\nDecember 21Last day of Fall 2026 Term \nWinter Holiday Break December 22 - January 4\nNote: the dates provided herein are academic dates as approved by NSCAD Senate. Financial refund dates are not reflected on this list. Please consult the T uition and \nFinancial Information (hyperlink https://nscad.ca/nscadfinancial/) website for important refund dates.\nIMPORTANTACADEMICSEMESTER DA26/27TES\nNSCAD  UNIVERSITY  98   ACADEMIC CATALOGUE  2024/25\nSummer Session A\nMay 03\nMay 04\nMay 11\nMay  18\nMay  24\nJune  \n28June \n21-25\nJuly 01\nSemester classes begin \nSenate Graduation Date (Administrative) \nConvocation Day  \nLast day to add a course\nVictoria Day - No Classes\nFaculty mid-session warning \nNo classes - Summer Study Break \nLast day to drop course with a “W” \nCanada Day - No classes \nNatal Day - No Classes August 2\nAugust 4\nAugust 5 -18\nAugust 24\nAugust 25\nSession classes begin May 03\nLast day to add/ drop a course May 10\nVictoria Day - No Classes May  24\nFaculty mid-session warning May 25\nLast day to drop course with a “W” May 31\nJune 14\n (noon) June \n15 - 21\nSession classes end\nAssessment Period\nLast day for grade submission\nLast day of Summer A Term\nSummer Session B\nSession classes begin July 05\nLast day to add/ drop a course July 12\nFaculty mid-session warning July 26\nLast day to drop course with a “W” August  02\nNatal Day - No classes \nAugust 24\nAugust 16Session classes end \nAssessment Period \nLast day for grade submission (noon)\nLast day of Summer B Term \nAugust 17 - 23\nWinter Semester\nJanuary 06\nJanuary 07\nJanuary 21\nFebruary 04\nFebruary  15\nFebruary 15-19\nFebruary 25\nMarch 04\nMarch 26\nMarch\n28\nApril 08\nApril 09\nApril \nApril \nWinter 2027 term begins\nOrientation - New Winter Students \nSemester classes begin \nLast day to add/drop a course without financial \nimpact \nCourses dropped recorded on transcript as “W” \nUniversity Closed - Heritage Day No classes - \nWinter Study Break \nFaculty mid-semester warning deadline\nLast day to drop course with a “W” \nUniversity Closed - Good Friday \nUniversity Closed - Easter Monday \nLast day of instruction for Monday classes \nLast Day of Classes \nAssessment period (flex/study/exams/ critiques) \nLast day for grade submission (noon)\nLast day of Winter 2027 Term April \n12-23\nSummerWinter \n2027 2027\n29\n30\nJune \n15\nLast day of instruction for Monday classes August 3\nSummer Full SemesterSummer Full Semester\nSummer Full Semester classes end (Thursday schedule)\nAssessment Period \nLast day for grade submission (noon)\nLast day of Summer T erm\nAugust 25\nJune \n23\nJune 25\nJanuary 05\nNSCAD  UNIVERSITY  1110   ACADEMIC CATALOGUE  2024/25\nLAND ACKNOWLEDGEMENT \nNSCAD University is located in Kjipuktuk, the district of \nSipekne’katik, Mi’kma’ki, which since time immemorial \nhas been the unceded traditional territory and ancestral \nhomelands of the Mi’kmaq Nation . We pay respect to the \nhistoric and contemporary Mi’kmaw artists who have, \nover millennia, created unique artforms and designs, and \nthrough generations, passed down ways of being, \nknowing, and doing that are valued and respected . \nNSCAD University is committed to forging a relationship \nbased on reciprocity as we move forward together in a \ngood way, based on the foundational aspirations in our \ncollective treaties, dating back to 1725 .  In Nova Scotia, \nwe recognize we are all T reaty People .\nPEOPLES’ \nACKNOWLEDGEMENT  \nNSCAD acknowledges the historical and contemporary \npresence of people of African descent . It offers this \nPeople’s Acknowledgement in solidarity with the \nIndigenous people of T urtle Island in the efforts and \ndeliberate intentions toward decolonization . African Nova \nScotians have existed and persisted on the traditional \nland of the Mi’kmaq for more than 400 years . We \nacknowledge and express deep gratitude . Their \nsignificant presence and contributions on this land have \ncontributed to the existence of Nova Scotia . Their labour \nand skills, used without their explicit consent and without \nreciprocity, have shaped the structural stability and were \ninstrumental in the creation of the intergenerational \nwealth of today’s Nova Scotia . They are resilient, brave, \nand spiritual and express pride in their Ancestors on \nwhose shoulders they stand . They are the founders of the \nmore than 50 historical African Nova Scotian communities \nacross Nova Scotia .\nThis acknowledgement brings with it a call to action to \nreconcile the harms acted upon African Nova Scotians, a \ndistinct People and the Mi’kmaw People .\nPURPOSE  \nThe purpose of the NSCAD University Academic Calendar \nis to provide information about programs and courses \noffered by the university . It includes matters of general \ninterest to students registered in a program at NSCAD, as \nwell as information on the policies that are relevant to a \nstudent’s progression through their program of study . \nThe Course Catalogue section of the Academic Calendar \nincludes descriptions of courses either regularly or \nrecently offered as part of NSCAD University’s degree and \ncertificate programs . Listing a course in the Course \nCatalogue does not guarantee that a particular course is \nbeing offered in a particular year . Students should consult \nthe official timetable of classes for a specific semester to \nview course offerings for that semester . The timetable of \nclasses is accessible for students through Self-Service, \nwhich is NSCAD University’s student information portal .\nStudents should note that the content of this publication \nis subject to change without notice . The Academic \nCalendar is published in advance of the academic year to \nwhich it relates . Changes will be posted on the NSCAD \nwebsite as soon as reasonably possible . Every student \naccepted for enrolment at NSCAD University is deemed to \nhave agreed to any such deletion, revision or addition \nwhether made before or after said acceptance . \nNotwithstanding the previous statement, a student’s \nprogram of study is governed by the requirements that \nexist at the time of a student’s entry into NSCAD \nUniversity and is subject to the availability of course \nofferings and residency requirements . Students are \nexpected to read the Academic Calendar carefully \nand accept responsibility for their academic goals \nand choices, as well as all academic regulations \nrelated to student life at NSCAD. \nNSCAD University does not accept any responsibility for \nloss or damage suffered or incurred by any student as a \nresult of suspension or termination of services or courses \ncaused by reason of strikes, lockouts, riots, weather, \ndamage to university property or for any other cause \nbeyond the reasonable control of the institution .\nInquiries should be directed to: \nThe Registrar Office\nOffice of Student Experience\nNSCAD University\n5163 Duke Street\nHalifax NS B3J 3J6\nT elephone:  (902) 494-2450\nFax:  (902) 494-1630 \nEmail:  registrar@nscad .ca\nAccurate as of July 1, 2025\nTHE OFFFICE OF STUDENT \nEXPERIENCE (OSE)  \nThe Office of Student Experience (OSE) is available to all \nNSCAD University students for support in areas of \nacademics, financial assistance, mental health, or \ncommunity involvement . Our office is designed to support \nNSCAD students academically, personally, and socially . \nThe OSE is comprised of three areas:\nOffice of Admissions & Recruitment\nThis unit plays a critical role in guiding aspiring artists and \ndesigners through the admission journey and showcasing \ncampus life, including: \n• Prospective student engagement \n• Recruitment, travel and events \n• Portfolio Day \n• Campus visits and tours\n• Application guidance and administration\nOffice of the Registrar\nThis unit maintains and administers matters related to \nstudent records, including: \n• Academic advising and program planning\n• Financial aid and awards administration\n• Student Loan guidance\n• T ranscript and confirmation of enrolment requests\n• Course selection\n• Graduation criteria\n• Institutional policies, procedures, and appeals\nOffice of Opportunity & Belonging\nThis unit supports wellness, belonging, and accessibility \nin the areas of:\n• Counselling & mental health: confidential short-term \ncounselling (in-person/virtual), peer-mentoring, \nworkshops, therapy dog visits, and community/\ntele-support partners like Good2T alk and Nova Scotia \nresources .\n• Student Engagement events and programming .\n• Accessibility services: accommodations for students \nwith disabilities (exam extensions, ASL interpreters, \nnotetakers), equipment funding, and referrals to \nspecialists . Students can register and renew \nterm-by-term .\n• International student support, housing guidance, \ndisclosures of bullying/ sexual violence support\n• Community-building events .\n• Disclosures of bullying/ sexual violence support .\nStudents are encouraged to visit the OSE on the second \nfloor of the Fountain Campus on Duke Street any time \nthey have questions or require support during their \nstudies at NSCAD .\nOffice of Career and Experiential \nLearning\nThis unit is a resource for student and faculty to explore \nopportunities for curricular experiential learning as part \nof courses or programs as well as career advising for \nNSCAD students.\nAcademic Advising\nAcademic Advisors are available throughout a student’s \njourney to offer information and advice to students when \nmaking registration decisions. This helps ensure \nstudents are registered for the courses required to \nprogress through their degree program, and to meet the \nminimum registration thresholds needed to offer the \ncourse . \n• New students: T he registration process for all new \nstudents includes academic advising . Newly \nadmitted students will be contacted by the Office of \nthe Registrar starting in mid-April to offer personal \nattention, support, and guidance during the \nregistration process .\n• Continuing students: Academic Advisors are \navailable advise on how a continuing student might \ndevelop a plan of study leading to graduation . \nAcademic advising is available for current students \nby appointment through the Registrar’s Office . \nProgram chairs may also serve as advisors and can \nhelp students make academic choices based on their \ninterests, aspirations and demonstrated skills and \nabilities . Regardless of advice and information \nprovided by program chairs or academic advisors, \nstudents are solely responsible for the curriculum \nchoices they make . Students who are approaching \nthe completion of 90 credits in their degree program \nshould schedule an appointment with their Academic \nAdvisor at least one year prior to their anticipated \ngraduation date to ensure they have chosen the \ncorrect courses to meet their degree requirements .\nDEFINITIONS  \nThe definitions below are for internal purposes only . \nExternal organizations (student loan providers, \nscholarship organizations, collective agreements, etc .) \nmay use different definitions for these terms . These \ndefinitions should be confirmed with the external \norganization prior to entering into any agreements . \nNSCAD  UNIVERSITY  1312   ACADEMIC CATALOGUE  2024/25\nAcademic Workload (Time on Task)\nUndergraduate students are expected to spend a \nminimum of three (3) hours per week working on their \nacademic studies for every credit of enrolment, including \nclass meeting time . \nFor example, if a student is registered for a 3-credit \ncourse, they can reasonably expect to spend 9 hours per \nweek on course work for that class between attending \nclass and working outside the regularly scheduled class \ntime .\nStudents registered in a full-time course load in a given \nsemester (15 credits) can reasonably expect to spend 45 \nhours per week on course work between attending class \nand working outside the regularly scheduled class time .\nGraduate students are expected to work beyond this \nguideline .\nUndergraduate student\nAn individual who has completed the formal admission \nprocedure and has been admitted to a Bachelor of Arts, \nBachelor of Fine Arts, or Bachelor of Design program at \nNSCAD University .\nCertificate student\nAn individual who has completed the formal admission \nprocedure and has been admitted to a Visual Arts \nCertificate or Post Baccalaureate Certificate program at \nNSCAD University .\nGraduate student\nAn individual who has completed formal admission \nprocedures and has been admitted to a Master of Art in \nArt Education, Master of Design, or Master of Fine Arts \nprogram at NSCAD University .\nVisiting student\nAn individual enrolled at another post-secondary \ninstitution who is permitted to take courses at NSCAD \nUniversity .\nNon-degree student\nAn individual with permission to take a course or courses \nfor credit at NSCAD University .  A non-degree student may \ncomplete up to 30 credits under this designation .\nAcademic Terms\nA defined period of time during which a college or \nuniversity holds classes and conducts its academic \ninstruction . At NSCAD University, our Academic T erms are \ndefined as follows:\n• Fall (September to December)\n• Winter (January to April)\n• Summer (May to August)\n• Summer A (May and June)\n• Summer B (July and August)\nFull-time student\nThe definition of full-time versus part time studies is \ndependent on a student’s program of study and the term \nin which they are studying . In general:\n• During the Fall, Winter, and Summer (May to August) \nterm, a student who is registered in at least 60% (9 \ncredits) of a full course load .\n• During the Fall, Winter, and Summer (May to August) \nterm, a student with a recognized disability as \ndetermined by the Accessibility Specialist who is \nregistered in at least 40% (6 credits) of a full course \nload .\n• In the MAED program, a full-"
